{"author":{"id":"746a4d30afc20b78119cb8ed7c194f988174fc252f0e9d9eb92022244ef86db3"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1779231793,"nonce":"+jwAJcsbAOGj7BMT1QoxlIIJlAs=","metadata":{"github-id":"I_kwDOK4fCvM8AAAABCyGGDg","github-url":"https://github.com/rolker/unh_echoboats_project11/issues/142","origin":"github"},"title":"Host thermal diagnostics: integrate lm-sensors + NVMe SMART as DiagnosticStatus on gabby + salmon","message":"## Goal\n\nSurface host-level thermals (CPU package, NVMe SMART, etc.) as `DiagnosticStatus` so they show up alongside MikroTik / Starlink / Teltonika network diagnostics in `/diagnostics_agg` — and become **bag-recordable / reviewable post-mission** rather than only visible to an operator with a shell open.\n\n## Today's relevance (2026-05-19 BizzyBoat deployment, gabby log §10–§11)\n\n- Hot day (93°F).\n- gabby CPU package: 67–70°C (low end of \"warm but fine\" for Intel).\n- **NVMe Sensor 1: 71.8°C** — inside the consumer-SSD thermal-throttle band (~70–75°C). Bag recording writes to that NVMe; if it throttles, manifests as bag growth-rate drop, not data loss.\n- Operator only saw these numbers because we polled `lm-sensors` from a shell on a 2-min cron during the deployment. Nothing automatic, nothing recorded.\n\n## Likely off-the-shelf\n\nA ROS package that wraps `lm-sensors` and/or `/sys/class/{thermal,hwmon}` and emits `DiagnosticStatus` probably already exists in the ROS 2 ecosystem. **First task** is to find it (search ROS Index / GitHub for terms like `lm_sensors`, `system_monitor`, `host_diagnostics`, `cpu_monitor`).\n\nCandidates to check (not verified):\n- `ros2_system_monitor` or similar\n- Whatever `cob_*` packages exist for ROS 2 (Care-o-bot had one in ROS 1)\n- NVMe-side: `smartctl` or `nvme smart-log` may need a separate small wrapper if no off-the-shelf node exposes SMART data\n\n## Scope\n\n1. **Find candidate package(s).** Compare against the sensors we care about (CPU package + per-core, NVMe SMART, memory pressure, optional fan/board temps).\n2. **Decide: integrate as-is, or write minimal wrapper.** Wrapper would be small — `lm-sensors` parsing is straightforward, NVMe `smartctl` parsing too.\n3. **Add to boat-side launch** (`bizzyboat_project11/launch/...`) so gabby publishes host thermals.\n4. **Add to operator-side launch** so salmon does the same.\n5. **Wire into diagnostics_aggregator config** so `/Boat/Host` and `/Operator/Host` (or similar) rollups show up in CAMP and rqt.\n6. **Add the new topics to boat-side + operator-side bag recording lists** in `bizzyboat.yaml` so it's reviewable post-mission.\n\n## Cross-platform\n\nThe wrapper choice should be platform-agnostic (Linux + `lm-sensors` + NVMe) so it can be used on izzy / future hosts without per-platform code. Per-platform tweaks only at the **integration layer** (which sensors matter, which thresholds, which launch file).\n\n## Related\n\n- 2026-05-19 deployment: [#135](https://github.com/rolker/unh_echoboats_project11/issues/135), gabby log §10 and §11.\n- Sibling pattern: existing network monitors in [`rolker/ros2_network_monitor`](https://github.com/rolker/ros2_network_monitor) (MikroTik / Teltonika / Starlink / Ping) — same shape: pull external system data, emit `DiagnosticStatus`. If we end up writing a wrapper, that repo would be a natural home for the *node* — but the *issue* lives here because the work is integration into BizzyBoat ops.\n\nPart of #135\n\n---\n**Authored-By**: `Claude Code Agent`\n**Model**: `Claude Opus 4.7 (1M context)`","files":null}]}